WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.023
(retro music)

00:00:01.023 --> 00:00:02.486
- [Voiceover] Pogs.

00:00:02.486 --> 00:00:05.272
You probably remember
this prolific 90s fad,

00:00:05.272 --> 00:00:06.761
but what you may not know is that

00:00:06.761 --> 00:00:07.778
Pogs have roots that go

00:00:07.778 --> 00:00:11.488
all the way back to 17th century Japan.

00:00:11.488 --> 00:00:13.988
(retro music)

00:00:15.124 --> 00:00:16.263
Okay, first thing's first.

00:00:16.263 --> 00:00:17.514
Let's review the rules.

00:00:17.514 --> 00:00:19.471
To play, you start with a stack of Pogs,

00:00:19.471 --> 00:00:20.419
all face down.

00:00:20.419 --> 00:00:22.753
Player one takes a slammer and
throw it down onto the pile

00:00:22.753 --> 00:00:25.254
and keeps any and all
Pogs that land face up.

00:00:25.254 --> 00:00:27.256
Pogs that remain face down are re-stacked,

00:00:27.256 --> 00:00:28.516
and the game continues on.

00:00:28.516 --> 00:00:31.249
The player with the most pogs
at the end of the game, wins.

00:00:31.249 --> 00:00:32.082
Hooray!

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:35.211
The origins of Pogs can
be traced back to this,

00:00:35.211 --> 00:00:37.479
Menko, a classic Japanese game.

00:00:37.479 --> 00:00:39.266
Players threw cards at
their opponents' cards

00:00:39.266 --> 00:00:40.752
with the goal of flipping them over.

00:00:40.752 --> 00:00:43.760
In the early 1900s, Japanese
immigrants settled in Hawaii

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:45.487
and brought the game with them.

00:00:45.487 --> 00:00:47.253
Over time, it evolved
into a version played with

00:00:47.253 --> 00:00:50.004
milk and juice caps from a
local dairy company in Maui,

00:00:50.004 --> 00:00:52.004
eventually renaming it
Pogs after their juice

00:00:52.004 --> 00:00:54.591
that was made out of passion
fruit, orange, and guava.

00:00:54.591 --> 00:00:55.471
Get it?

00:00:55.471 --> 00:00:57.769
In 1991, this woman, a sixth grade teacher

00:00:57.769 --> 00:00:58.980
named Blossom Galbiso,

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:00.738
introduced the game to her class.

00:01:00.738 --> 00:01:02.022
Her goal was to teach them math,

00:01:02.022 --> 00:01:04.749
but in a fun way, and it worked big time.

00:01:04.749 --> 00:01:07.767
Kids loved it, and the
game exploded in popularity

00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:10.501
throughout Hawaii and then
spread to the mainland.

00:01:10.501 --> 00:01:12.011
As the game took off,

00:01:12.011 --> 00:01:14.501
this marketing guy, Alan
Rypinski, took note.

00:01:14.501 --> 00:01:16.244
He purchased the trademark for Pogs,

00:01:16.244 --> 00:01:18.004
founded the World Pog Federation,

00:01:18.004 --> 00:01:20.483
and got them into every store in America.

00:01:20.483 --> 00:01:22.535
♫ Oom ma mow mow, Papa oom mow mow ♫

00:01:22.535 --> 00:01:25.767
And subsequently, into nearly
every home with a 90s kid.

00:01:25.767 --> 00:01:26.600
- [Voiceover] Pogs.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:27.931
- [Voiceover] Thanks, Alan.

00:01:27.931 --> 00:01:30.514
(upbeat music)

